{ The Grand Prix attack begins when the opponent plays the Sicilian. To begin, play e4 }
1. e4 c5 { Here you have two options: Nc3, or f4. Which one is better? } 2. Nc3 (2. f4 { If you play this, then the opponent can play d4, leading to some wild positions. }) 2... d6 { If the opponent plays the open Sicilian, then, it is good for us because they have committed to playing d6, instead of d5. Now play f4. } 3. f4 { This is the Grand Prix Attack. We will look at how to play this opening against different Sicilians. } *

[Event "Grand Prix Attack: Najdorf/Dragon"]
[Date "2021.06.18"]
[Result "*"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ECO "B93"]
[Opening "Sicilian Defense: Najdorf Variation, Amsterdam Variation"]
[StudyName "Grand Prix Attack"]
[ChapterName "Najdorf/Dragon"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/xbNM05RB/q4Jrq0AT"]
[Annotator "https://lichess.org/@/siddharth_08"]
[UTCDate "2021.06.18"]
[UTCTime "14:35:40"]
[Orientation "white"]
[ChapterMode "gamebook"]

1. e4 c5 2. Nc3 d6 3. f4 Nf6 4. Nf3 a6 { If they decide to play the Najdorf, then, we must take full control of the center } 5. d4 cxd4 6. Nxd4 Nc6 7. Be3 g6 { The opponent decides to add an extra defender to the kingside. Now you need your bishop to be on this diagonal. } { [%cal Gf3a8] } 8. Be2 Bd7 (8... Bg7 9. Bf3 Bd7 { And your queen must be on this diagonal } { [%cal Ge2b5,Ge2h5] } 10. Qe2 O-O 11. O-O-O) 9. Bf3 Bg7 { Qe2 } 10. Qe2 Qc7 11. O-O-O O-O { From here you will play as shown in the arrows } { [%cal Gg2g4,Gh2h4,Gd1f1,Gh1g1,Gf4f5] } *

[Event "Grand Prix Attack: Scheveningen Sicilian"]
[Date "2021.06.18"]
[Result "*"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ECO "B23"]
[Opening "Sicilian Defense: Closed"]
